': 1887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac89ca34-17b2-58a0-b21e-a7bdb23b82e9'}, {'abstract': 'In exploring the contradictions between private and public, interior and exterior, constructed and natural, Sou Fujimoto has offered his own definition for what design should be.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/02/t-magazine/sou-fujimoto.html', 'snippet': 'In exploring the contradictions between private and public, interior and exterior, constructed and natural, Sou Fujimoto has offered his own definition for what design should be.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE MOST IMPORTANT thing that Sou Fujimoto did as a young architect was to not produce any architecture. A transplant from rural Hokkaido, in the north of Japan, he was 23 years old in 1994 and had just graduated from the University of Tokyo, where he had studied architecture and had been impressed by the pure, elemental visions of Le Corbusier and Mies van der Rohe. But the work that seized his imagination nonetheless left him lost. He felt that he had learned too little in his time. There were so many possible directions for architecture that they were difficult for him to sort out. He was, by his own account, intensely shy, unwilling to brave the gauntlet of auditioning for Toyo Ito and Sanaa, Japanese architects and firms he admired.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '90', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/02/t-magazine/02tmag-fujimoto-slide-YTWT/02tmag-fujimoto-slide-YTWT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/02/t-magazine/02tmag-fujimoto-slide-YTWT/02tmag-fujimoto-slide-YTWT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/02/t-magazine/02tmag-fujimoto-slide-YTWT/02tmag-fujimoto-sli