r and Catie Edmondson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Catie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Edmondson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87b09abc-9b36-5428-8978-ecfccd11c834', 'word_count': 397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87b09abc-9b36-5428-8978-ecfccd11c834'}, {'abstract': 'Brins, a jam company from Brooklyn, makes a lemon-saffron marmalade.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/02/dining/brins-jam-marmalade.html', 'snippet': 'Brins, a jam company from Brooklyn, makes a lemon-saffron marmalade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m a marmalade lover, and this one — from Brins, a small Brooklyn company that makes unusual flavors, including banana — has intriguing allure. A dulcet riff on preserved lemons, with a musky hint of saffron, it’s fine as is, but the website suggests using it in marinades for Easter lamb and Moroccan lemon chicken, and pairing it with Uplands Pleasant Ridge Reserve, one of my favorite cheeses. Or swirl it into rice pudding. Candice Ross, an architect and the company’s owner, is originally from Lake Charles, La. “Brin” means “a little bit” in Cajun French.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-jam/merlin_158490852_694c47d7-14f6-4675-b33d-58d0525e33df-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 828, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-jam/merlin_158490852_694c47d7-14f6-4675-b33d-58d0525e33df-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 828}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-jam/merlin_158490852_694c47d7-14f6-4675-b33d-58d0525e33df-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 743, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-jam/merlin_158490852_694c47d7-14f6-4675-b33d-58d0525e33df-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1485, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-jam/02burner-jam-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-jam/02burner-jam-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-jam/02burner-jam-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Marmalade With a Moroccan Touch', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Spread: A Moroccan Touch With This Marmalade', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citrus Fruits', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brins Jam and Marmalade', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-02T23:39:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b012f17-9a6c-5243-b51f-3b7dd1ae0584', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b012f17-9a6c-5243-b51f-3b7dd1ae0584'}, {'abstract': 'The tasty history of the Phoenicia Diner is recounted, with recipes, in a new cookbook.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/02/dining/phoenicia-diner-cookbook.html', 'snippet': 'The tasty history of the Phoenicia Diner is recounted, with recipes, in a new cookbook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Visitors to the Catskills are likely to be familiar with the Phoenicia Diner on Route 28 near Esopus Creek, known for fly-fishing and tubing. The diner is a classic in the genre; it opened in 1962, was moved to its present location in the 1980s and fell on hard times until Mike Cioffi, who was in scenery construction for shows, bought it, fixed it up and reopened it in 2012. The cookbook reflects the diversity of the diner menu, and of the region. There are a number of Mexican dishes, alongside soft scrambled eggs with smoked trout from the chapter on breakfast all day; kimchi in sandwiches; and several recipes for grits, thanks to the chef, Chris Bradley from North Carolina, who is also an author. Some dishes call for local nettles, mushrooms and ramps. Essays on moonshine, the borscht belt and folk music are included.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-book/merlin_169582626_f8b46c38-005f-4e5b-9858-ad09a13197d7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 806, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-book/merlin_169582626_f8b46c38-005f-4e5b-9858-ad09a13197d7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 806}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-book/merlin_169582626_f8b46c38-005f-4e5b-9858-ad09a13197d7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 763, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-book/merlin_169582626_f8b46c38-005f-4e5b-9858-ad09a13197d7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1525, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-book/02burner-book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-book/02burner-book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-book/02burner-book-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Secrets of a Catskills Diner', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Consult: A Volume of Secrets From a Catskills Diner', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diners (Eating Places)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Esopus Creek (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Phoenicia Diner (Phoenicia, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Phoenicia Diner Cookbook: Dishes and Dispatches From the Catskill Mountains (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-02T23:40:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c38b067-a5ca-5f94-86b4-d2ef52e490e0', 'word_count': 194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c38b067-a5ca-5f94-86b4-d2ef52e490e0'}, {'abstract': 'A new vendor at Turnstyle, in the Columbus Circle station, sells a rainbow of Dutch cheese flavors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/02/dining/gouda-2dutcheese.html', 'snippet': 'A new vendor at Turnstyle, in the Columbus Circle station, sells a rainbow of Dutch cheese flavors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The international array of vendors of dumplings, sandwiches and sweets in the Turnstyle market at the 59th Street-Columbus Circle subway station now includes a stand from the Netherlands, selling cheese. As a student in California, Younes Shina, who is from Rotterdam, missed his hometown Goudas. Along with a partner, he now imports and sells them in assorted styles and flavors. His stand is in the center of the market, and samples of all the cheeses are available for tasting. There are straightforward, slightly nutty young and aged varieties, along with some made from goat milk. Others are seasoned with coconut, guacamole, truffles and chestnuts. But despite the offbeat ingredients and colors, like green, it’s the taste of the Gouda that comes through. The cheeses are sold in 8-ounce wedges, precut and wrapped.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-cheese/merlin_169593405_62c11bdf-8fa1-4c25-92a0-a7e0ae151180-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-cheese/merlin_169593405_62c11bdf-8fa1-4c25-92a0-a7e0ae151180-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-cheese/merlin_169593405_62c11bdf-8fa1-4c25-92a0-a7e0ae151180-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-cheese/merlin_169593405_62c11bdf-8fa1-4c25-92a0-a7e0ae151180-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-cheese/02burner-cheese-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-cheese/02burner-cheese-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02burner-cheese/02burner-cheese-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Good Gouda, Head to the Subway', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Slice: Head to the Subway For Good Gouda', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Turnstyle (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheese', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stations and Terminals (Passenger)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Columbus Circle (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '2Dutcheese', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-02T23:42:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e86112e-7991-5e7e-9a39-33500f95ca21', 'word_count': 177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e86112e-7991-5e7e-9a39-33500f95ca21'}, {'abstract': 'Kajitsu hosts a wagashi-maker from Kyoto for an event celebrating spring.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/02/dining/kajitsu-wagashi-making.html', 'snippet': 'Kajitsu hosts a wagashi-maker from Kyoto for an event celebrating spring.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Japan, making intricate sweets called wagashi — usually with bean and rice pastes and molded or shaped like flowers — is an art. The confections are often served at tea ceremonies and given as gifts. Shioyoshi-ken, a shop that has been in business in Kyoto since 1882, is known for its wagashi mastery, which will be demonstrated by one of its owners this week in New York. The sweets will be available for purchase.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02Burner-wag/02Burner-wag-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02Burner-wag/02Burner-wag-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02Burner-wag/02Burner-wag-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/04/dining/02Burner-wag/0