nion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hartosh Singh Bal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hartosh', 'middlename': 'Singh', 'lastname': 'Bal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e359240e-7adb-5b3b-9ec9-4c9742a7f42a', 'word_count': 1232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e359240e-7adb-5b3b-9ec9-4c9742a7f42a'}, {'abstract': 'Xi Jinping’s handling of the epidemic is reviving political dissent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/03/opinion/coronavirus-china-xi-jinping.html', 'snippet': 'Xi Jinping’s handling of the epidemic is reviving political dissent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Chinese Communist Party has always been quick to congratulate itself for how it deals with crises, be they natural disasters or catastrophes of its own making. The coronavirus epidemic is no exception, even now that it has become a global health emergency. The government of China’s first response to the deadly virus, detected in late December, was dilatory at best, willfully negligent at worst, and yet the party promptly lavished praise on the state, particularly on China’s president, Xi Jinping.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03barmeWeb/03barmeWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 446, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03barmeWeb/03barmeWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 446}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03barmeWeb/03barmeWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 761, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03barmeWeb/03barmeWeb-supe