ticle', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Gordon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fcdead4-ca67-53d3-bb95-8366bc8b2105', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fcdead4-ca67-53d3-bb95-8366bc8b2105'}, {'abstract': 'Start with the right mix of local seeds, clay and compost. Get permission before bombing the land.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/03/magazine/how-to-make-a-wildflower-bomb.html', 'snippet': 'Start with the right mix of local seeds, clay and compost. Get permission before bombing the land.', 'lead_paragraph': '“You can throw them out of a moving car, from a bicycle, on a hike,” says Daniel Cunningham, a horticulturist at Texas A&M, who has conducted preliminary trials on bomb-making techniques that lead to higher rates of seed germination. Before you go launching wildflower seed projectiles, start with a solid recipe. You’ll need a mixing bowl and baking sheets. Add one part native wildflower seed mix — Cunningham’s include bluebonnets, blanket flowers and native grasses, but yours should reflect what grows endemic to your region — to four parts powdered clay and five parts fine-grained compost. Mix the dry ingredients thoroughly and stir in water slowly until you have a thick, bread-dough-like consistency.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08Mag-Tip-01/08Mag-Tip-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 635, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08Mag-Tip-01/08Mag-Tip-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 635}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08Mag-Tip-01/08Mag-Tip-01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 842, 'width': 796, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08Mag-Tip-01/08Mag-Tip-01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 842, 'width': 796, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08Mag-Tip-01/08Mag-Tip-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08Mag-Tip-01/08Mag-Tip-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08Mag-Tip-01/08Mag-Tip-01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Make a Wildflower Bomb', 'kicker': 'Tip ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Make a Wildflower Bomb', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-03T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c600537a-3310-5d6e-843c-fc711af26888', 'word_count': 386, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c600537a-3310-5d6e-843c-fc711af26888'}, {'abstract': 'He’s an auteur whose bizarre creations — the Metal Gear Solid series and, most recently, Death Stranding — have become huge blockbusters. Why do gamers find them so captivating?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/03/magazine/hideo-kojima-death-stranding-video-game.html', 'snippet': 'He’s an auteur whose bizarre creations — the Metal Gear Solid series and, most recently, Death Stranding — have become huge blockbusters. Why do gamers find them so captivating?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Death Stranding, the newest video game from the developer Hideo Kojima, was introduced to the world in the summer of 2016 on a stage at the Electronic Entertainment Expo (E3), the gaming industry’s annual trade-show extravaganza. The event was what the industry calls a press conference, but this particular spectacle more closely resembled the scene in “King Kong” when the beast is displayed to the public in a Broadway theater. A live orchestra struck up a blaring John Williams-style score as the curtain lifted to reveal a sloping stage, at the very top of which stood Kojima, a slight Japanese man with swooping black hair and glasses. The floor began to light up underneath him, panes of light flying in from left and right to create a sort of light bridge, which he bounded down to shake a Sony executive’s hand. Then he stood at center stage, beaming, to deafening applause.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '37', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08mag-kojima/08mag-kojima-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 849, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08mag-kojima/08mag-kojima-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 849}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08mag-kojima/08mag-kojima-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 724, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08mag-kojima/08mag-kojima-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1448, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08mag-kojima/08mag-kojima-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08mag-kojima/08mag-kojima-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08mag-kojima/08mag-kojima-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hideo Kojima’s Strange, Unforgettable Video-Game Worlds', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Auteur', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kojima, Hideo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-03T10:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adrian Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adrian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a957fe3-d75b-5bc1-bcda-8aed847fd274', 'word_count': 7772, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a957fe3-d75b-5bc1-bcda-8aed847fd274'}, {'abstract': 'We live in an interconnected world, where borders are porous, more like living membranes than physical walls.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/03/opinion/the-coronavirus-is-us.html', 'snippet': 'We live in an interconnected world, where borders are porous, more like living membranes than physical walls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The new coronavirus (COVID-19) outbreak, now threatening to snowball into a full-fledged pandemic, has already begun to wreak a sort of global havoc, causing alarm and even panic in several countries, spasms in financial markets and, most painfully, loss of life. There has been very little time so far for reflection, and few of us have stopped to wonder what this crisis might tell us about ourselves — about our bodies, our communities, our political systems and the nature of our growing interconnectedness across borders. But I believe it has something crucial to tell.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03stone-marder/03stone-marder-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03stone-marder/03stone-marder-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03stone-marder/03stone-marder-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03stone-marder/03stone-marder-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03stone-marder/03stone-marder-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03stone-marder/03stone-marder-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03stone-marder/03stone-marder-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Coronavirus Is Us', 'kicker': 'THE STONE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Epidemics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-03T10:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Marder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d7a4d60-81ba-5234-8844-15f5ca4af89c', 'word_count': 1033, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d7a4d60-81ba-5234-8844-15f5ca4af89c'}, {'abstract': 'What responsibility do social media sites like YouTube have to make sure the videos they promote meet some standard for truth? Or, do you think it’s solely the responsibility of viewers to verify the accuracy of claims?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/03/learning/do-you-think-online-conspiracy-theories-can-be-dangerous.html', 'snippet': 'What responsibility do social media sites like YouTube have to make sure the videos they promote meet some standard for truth? Or, do you think it’s solely the responsibility of viewers to verify the accuracy of claims?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do you believe in any conspiracy theories? Have you seen conspiracy theories featured in videos, memes, podcasts or talk shows? How do you know when to quickly dismiss a theory as unfounded or absurd — and when to consider if there’s any truth behind it?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/02/21/video/xxvid-youtube-loop-still/xxvid-youtube-loop-still-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/02/21/video/xxvid-youtube-loop-still/xxvid-youtube-loop-still-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/02/21/video/xxvid-youtube-loop-still/xxvid-youtube-loop-still-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/02/21/video/xxvid-youtube-loop-still/xxvid-youtube-loop-still-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/02/21/video/xxvid-youtube-loop-still/xxvid-youtube-loop-still-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/02/21/video/xxvid-youtube-loop-still/xxvid-youtube-loop-still-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/02/21/video/xxvid-youtube-loop-still/xxvid-youtube-loop-still-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Do You Think Online Conspiracy Theories Can Be Dangerous?', 'kicker': 'student opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conspiracy Theories', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-03T10:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Daniels', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Daniels', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/115d6e22-3429-51e1-9462-497d9ba35456', 'word_count': 885, 'uri': 'nyt://article/115d6e22-3429-51e1-9462-497d9ba35456'}, {'abstract': 'It’s a good idea to be on speaking terms with bad luck.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/03/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-gambling.html', 'snippet': 'It’s a good idea to be on speaking terms with bad luck.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the sunny June day my father got remarried 25 years ago, just a relatively short time before the ceremony, I was at Belmont Park betting horses with him and his friend Bruce, a gentle soul who, for his keen and obsessively practiced handicapping skills, had been given (by my father) the tongue-in-cheek nickname the Shark. I’m a punctual, anxious person, so I reminded Dad that the nuptials approached. He assured me we would leave after the next race.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/magazine/08Mag-LOR-1/08Mag-LOR-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 437, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge'