20/03/03/opinion/abortion-supreme-court.html', 'snippet': 'The most sophisticated opponents of abortion have long been playing a much subtler game — and we’re about to see it come to fruition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What if the heartbeat bills — those bans on abortion as early as six weeks into a pregnancy that made headlines last spring in states from Georgia to Missouri — were not the real story? What if the most serious threat to Roe v. Wade were far more subtle — and based on arguments similar to those that the Supreme Court has already rejected?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/02/opinion/02ziegler1/merlin_167732124_77411c3e-0e20-4c31-af60-4bdf9938158d-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/02/opinion/02ziegler1/merlin_167732124_77411c3e-0e20-4c31-af60-4bdf9938158d-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/02/opinion/02ziegler1/merlin_167732124_77411c3e-0e20-4c31-af60-4bdf9938158d-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/02/opinion/02ziegler1/merlin_167732124_77411c3e-0e20-4c31-af60-4bdf9938158d-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/02/opinion/02ziegler1/02ziegler1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/02/opinion/02ziegler1/02ziegler1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/02/opinion/02ziegler1/02ziegler1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Heartbeat Bills Were Never the Real Threat to Abortion Rights', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roe v Wade (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's Rights", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Louisiana', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-03T10:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Ziegler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ziegler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/21989e5b-2271-5b8b-b016-d3da4284e162', 'word_count': 1120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21989e5b-2271-5b8b-b016-d3da4284e162'}, {'abstract': 'Alice Notley’s new book-length epic, “For the Ride,” follows a figure struggling to figure out the terms of a strange new world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/03/books/review/for-the-ride-alice-notley.html', 'snippet': 'Alice Notley’s new book-length epic, “For the Ride,” follows a figure struggling to figure out the terms of a strange new world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Really great poetry is difficult to read. I don’t just mean it’s challenging, though it usually is. I mean it’s hard to make progress, because the density of meaning in the language stops you; it makes you read in loops. Alice Fulton has called poetry “recursive”: “It sends you back up the page as much as it sends you forward.” Because of this effect, it once took me all afternoon to finish reading John Ashbery’s long poem “Self-Portrait in a Convex Mirror” — I kept wanting to stop and start over again. Alice Notley’s best work feels this way: intensely recursive, almost too good to read. In its semantic density, great poetry gives you the sense you’ve skipped over and missed some available shade of meaning. You certainly have.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/04/05/books/05Gabbert1/Gabbert1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/04/05/books/05Gabbert1/Gabbert1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/04/05/books/05Gabbert1/Gabbert1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 983, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/04/05/books/05Gabbert1/Gabbert1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 983, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/04/05/books/05Gabbert1/Gabbert1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/04/05/books/05Gabbert1/Gabbert1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/04/05/books/05Gabbert1/Gabbert1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Defense of Poetic Nonsense, With a Character Who Shares Your Frustration', 'kicker': 'On Poetry', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lost in the Glyph', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Notley, Alice', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'For the Ride (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-03T10:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisa Gabbert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabbert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/39b521d5-5ebe-5425-b0ba-f77971b4cc64', 'word_count': 1247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39b521d5-5ebe-5425-b0ba-f77971b4cc64'}, {'abstract': 'On the ballot in Los Angeles County is the trajectory of a national movement to reshape an unjust criminal justice system.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/03/opinion/california-super-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'On the ballot in Los Angeles County is the trajectory of a national movement to reshape an unjust criminal justice system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Tucked into the hundreds of millions of dollars that have bought Michael Bloomberg admission to the presidential sweepstakes is an incongruous silver lin