8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Minnesota', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-03T10:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2cc3f403-9c03-5f0e-bb0c-097e83c11d4b', 'word_count': 1987, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2cc3f403-9c03-5f0e-bb0c-097e83c11d4b'}, {'abstract': 'A case before the court could expand the president’s power to remove their leaders.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/03/opinion/seila-law-supreme-court.html', 'snippet': 'A case before the court could expand the president’s power to remove their leaders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Trump has repeatedly used his office to further his personal political interests. But so far, he has been unable to interfere with the “independent agencies,” those parts of the government intended by Congress to operate outside of the president’s direct authority. A case set to be argued at the Supreme Court Tuesday threatens to change this.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03MannersMenand/merlin_161373675_32126e66-7f67-46bc-877c-72bfc04a9373-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03MannersMenand/merlin_161373675_32126e66-7f67-46bc-877c-72bfc04a9373-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/03/opinion/03MannersMenand/merlin_161373675_32126e66-7f67-46bc-877c-72bfc04a9373-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumb