es (Play)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Perplexed (Play)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-03T14:20:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kurt Soller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kurt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/951bf798-1c8a-51b2-9d36-e58d8afc7e90', 'word_count': 3112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/951bf798-1c8a-51b2-9d36-e58d8afc7e90'}, {'abstract': 'Years of milder temperatures have made German ice wine increasingly rare and expensive. This year, the industry body says, there will be only a few bottles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/03/world/europe/ice-wine-germany.html', 'snippet': 'Years of milder temperatures have made German ice wine increasingly rare and expensive. This year, the industry body says, there will be only a few bottles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — The tradition has persisted for nearly two centuries in what is now Germany, through wars, epidemics and revolutions. Before dawn on the bitterest of winter mornings, vintners harvest grapes that are frozen on the vine, pressing them before they thaw to produce a small quantity of exquisitely concentrated sweet white wine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/03/world/03Germany-IceWine01/03Germany-IceWine01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/03/world/03Germany-IceWine01/03Germany-IceWine01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/03/world/03Germany-Ic