 the Lane (Play)', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': '& Juliet (Play)', 'rank': 19, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-03T15:56:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Marshall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marshall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e353ea0a-6812-5e70-b3c8-aa8568ec5267', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e353ea0a-6812-5e70-b3c8-aa8568ec5267'}, {'abstract': 'Jeremy Anderson, the co-founder of the modernist lighting studio Apparatus, is molding a space for himself with a collection of porcelain and stoneware vessels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/03/t-magazine/jeremy-anderson-ceramics.html', 'snippet': 'Jeremy Anderson, the co-founder of the modernist lighting studio Apparatus, is molding a space for himself with a collection of porcelain and stoneware vessels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the designer Jeremy Anderson, 44, was a kid growing up in suburban Minneapolis, his favorite toys were paper dolls, though he rarely played with them. “It wasn’t something little boys did,” he remembers. “It was this secret thing I loved.” Decades later, Anderson traces a link between that furtive interest and his heretofore behind-the-scenes ceramics practice, throwing chubby anthropomorphic vessels that he “dresses up” with hand-painted stripes and raised ridges that bend and vibrate to trippy effect.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '59', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/02/t-magazine/02tmag-ceramics/02tmag-ceramics-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/0