N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-05T10:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter S. Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c9f768d-afef-5912-9ff9-d96cc0460cb7', 'word_count': 1571, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c9f768d-afef-5912-9ff9-d96cc0460cb7'}, {'abstract': 'An excerpt from “The Exhibition of Persephone Q,” by Jessi Jezewska Stevens', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/05/books/review/the-exhibition-of-persephone-q-by-jessi-jezewska-stevens-an-excerpt.html', 'snippet': 'An excerpt from “The Exhibition of Persephone Q,” by Jessi Jezewska Stevens', 'lead_paragraph': 'One night, I woke up to find I no longer recognized my husband. The room was dark and still and blue. The computer in the kitchen glowed. I had woken, as I sometimes do, in a fit of desire that was usually reciprocated, as if Misha and I were wired to the same internal alarm: we woke up as one. This was often how we began to make love. We reached for each other through the film of separate dreams and then fell back in states of mutual surprise, nonverbally reaffirmed. That night, however, he slept. The thin light spilled through the air shaft and onto our bed, and in it he looked strange. Not less attractive—if anything, more so—but also not quite like the man I’d married not so long ago. His chin was slack. His brow furrowed with some small concern. I rubbed my eyes. Misha was older than I was by only a few months, but in that moment it seemed a whole decade had intervened. I reached out to touch his cheek across a stretch of time. He didn’t stir.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main':