view', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6297bcf-e229-59bc-8953-1e0273835491', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6297bcf-e229-59bc-8953-1e0273835491'}, {'abstract': '“You Call This Democracy?” shows the rising generation how our government falls short of its democratic ideals. And what they can do about it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/06/books/review/you-call-this-democracy-elizabeth-rusch.html', 'snippet': '“You Call This Democracy?” shows the rising generation how our government falls short of its democratic ideals. And what they can do about it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The recent Iowa caucus debacle could serve as a primer on America’s not-quite-representative system of government. The malfunctioning app. The reporting delays and inconsistencies. The delegates determined by coin tosses and names in hats, the confusing first and second alignments, the discrepancy between who got the most delegates and who got the most votes, the built-in barriers to access created by having to stand in a gym for hours at a specific time of a specific day. Is this really the best we can do?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/books/review/08-BKS-MOSER/08-BKS-MOSER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/08/books/review/08-BKS-MOSER/08-BKS-MOSER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/books/review/08-BKS-MOSER/08-BKS-MOSER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/books/review/08-BKS-MOSER/08-BKS-MOSER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/books/review/08-BKS-MOSER/08-BKS-MOSER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/08/books/review/08-BKS-MOSER/08-BKS-MOSER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/books/review/08-BKS-MOSER/08-BKS-MOSER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Teenagers Fix Our Government?', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Can Teenagers Fix Our Government?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'You Call This Democracy?: How to Fix Our Government and Deliver Power to the People (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rusch, Elizabeth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-06T18:20:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Moser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab38d834-36f2-5e2f-9642-0a6adb470d6b', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab38d834-36f2-5e2f-9642-0a6adb470d6b'}, {'abstract': 'In “Stamped,” Ibram X. Kendi and Jason Reynolds team up to help teenagers understand — and combat — racism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/06/books/review/stamped-ibram-x-kendi-jason-reynolds.html', 'snippet': 'In “Stamped,” Ibram X. Kendi and Jason Reynolds team up to help teenagers understand — and combat — racism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Americans have always assumed that the key to undoing the all-encompassing framework of white supremacy lies, in part, in literacy. Literacy helped combat slavery in practical ways — reading and writing meant you could more easily escape by forging free papers, for example. But there was also literacy’s metaphysical promise — that you could write your way into new futures, into places that existed outside of imaginations made narrow by anti-blackness. Think of Frederick Douglass tricking white boys into teaching him to form letters, or Toni Morrison proudly displaying a letter from the Texas Board of Corrections declaring that it had banned her novel “Paradise,” because of its potential to start a riot. Books and reading are dangerous, which is why the question of which books are made available to children is always contested.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/books/review/08-BKS-GREENIDGE/08-BKS-GREENIDGE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/08/books/review/08-BKS-GREENIDGE/08-BKS-GREENIDGE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subty