None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-08T05:10:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdc3bb4f-6109-5eea-aafa-4050bad29187', 'word_count': 252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdc3bb4f-6109-5eea-aafa-4050bad29187'}, {'abstract': 'Catch the season finales of Jim Carrey’s dark comedy “Kidding” on Showtime and the Stephen King adaptation “The Outsider” on HBO.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/08/arts/television/whats-on-tv-sunday-kidding-and-the-outsider.html', 'snippet': 'Catch the season finales of Jim Carrey’s dark comedy “Kidding” on Showtime and the Stephen King adaptation “The Outsider” on HBO.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KIDDING 10 p.m. on Showtime. When a show is granted a second run, it faces the pressure of matching the success of its first and proving it has promise for more to come. This poignant tragicomedy, about a beloved children’s television host (Jim Carrey) who strains to keep his brand intact as his personal life crumbles, has plenty of life left in it. Last week’s episode walked us through the past, before Jeff’s son Phil died in a car accident. Season 2 wraps up on Sunday with a two-part finale, in which Jeff realizes he would be better off on his own for a while. If you have yet to give the show a try, it’s never too late to delve into the dark, imaginative world of Puppet Time — and Carrey puts on an emotional, layered performance.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/08/arts/08tvcol-kidding/08tvcol-kidding-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'leg