son': [{'firstname': 'Maggie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Astor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Shane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldmacher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stevens', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea8bdec3-77ad-567a-84c1-924fe9459244', 'word_count': 2717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea8bdec3-77ad-567a-84c1-924fe9459244'}, {'abstract': 'Black and white day dresses, almost saintly in their simplicity, commune with strappy sandals and lace gloves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/10/t-magazine/spring-fashion-romantic-look.html', 'snippet': 'Black and white day dresses, almost saintly in their simplicity, commune with strappy sandals and lace gloves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Now considered quintessentially Provençal, the southern French city of Arles was once a part of the Roman Empire, and many relics of its ancient past are still standing. In 1981, the city center was even named a Unesco World Heritage Site on account of its Roman and Romanesque monuments. Among them is the Roman Catholic Church of St. Trophime, which was built between the 12th and 15th centuries, and the Arles Amphitheater, where, in medieval times, gladiators and charioteers entertained tens of thousands of spectators at a time. The Amphitheater, along with the nearby first-century Théâtre Antique, proved fitting locations for T’s latest digital cover shoot, which was photographed by Pieter Hugo and styled by Jay Massacret. The looks feature billowing black and white dresses and separates with nipped-in waists and ecclesiastical touches like high collars and lace. On their feet the models wore gladiator-style sandals — another nod to the last millennium.', 'sour