 {'original': 'By United Labor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'United', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Labor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/01373eea-32cb-5add-9843-503ec5f79de3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/01373eea-32cb-5add-9843-503ec5f79de3'}, {'abstract': 'Katie Stout is the latest subject to take our “Make T Something” challenge.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/11/t-magazine/katie-stout.html', 'snippet': 'Katie Stout is the latest subject to take our “Make T Something” challenge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Brooklyn-based designer Katie Stout creates imaginative, unconventional housewares that often eschew utility. In fact, her pieces — which include dramatically spiked ceramic lamps and floppy chairs made from brightly hued fabric wrapped in vinyl — “could potentially cause more trouble” to live with than not, she says with a laugh. She has also become known for her ongoing series of cartoonlike “girls,” candy-colored ceramic sculptures of nude women performing various tasks, such as holding up lampshades or mirrors, that playfully call attention to the multiple roles women are often expected to take on in domestic spaces.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/11/t-magazine/11tmag-katie-stout/11tmag-katie-stout-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/11/t-magazine/11tmag-katie-stout/11tmag-katie-stout-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/11/t-magazine/11tmag-katie-stout/11tmag-katie-stout-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType