esse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wegman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d3a567e-0f34-5c62-822f-cd95c667aea4', 'word_count': 2128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d3a567e-0f34-5c62-822f-cd95c667aea4'}, {'abstract': 'One time, mid-announcement the captain actually said, “I know you probably already heard this on the news.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/13/opinion/who-was-on-coronavirus-cruise.html', 'snippet': 'One time, mid-announcement the captain actually said, “I know you probably already heard this on the news.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m not sure when it came. I’m not exactly sure of the time or date or precise location in which the virus entered my body. I don’t know how long it was there or when exactly my body started to fight it. All I know is that 67 days after my wife and I packed our bags and left Utah for vacation, I found myself in quarantine on the Diamond Princess cruise ship, at a port in Yokohama, Japan, on Feb. 3; that six days later I’d come down with an intense fever and extreme nausea; and that on Valentine’s Day I would test positive for the new coronavirus.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/12/opinion/00haering1/00haering1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/12/opinion/00haering1/00haering1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 383}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/12/opinion/00haering1/00haering1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 654, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/12/opinion/00haering1/00haering1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1307, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/12/opinion/00haering1/00haering1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/12/opinion/00haering1/00haering1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/12/opinion/00haering1/00haering1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What It’s Like to Get Coronavirus on a Cruise Ship', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-13T09:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Haering', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haering', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d11e1a3f-0637-55af-b4ae-ced5c011a9a2', 'word_count': 1615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d11e1a3f-0637-55af-b4ae-ced5c011a9a2'}, {'abstract': 'At the molecular level, soap breaks things apart. At the level of society, it helps hold everything together.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/13/health/soap-coronavirus-handwashing-germs.html', 'snippet': 'At the molecular level, soap breaks things apart. At the level of society, it helps hold everything together.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It probably began with an accident thousands of years ago. According to one legend, rain washed the fat and ash from frequent animal sacrifices into a nearby river, where they formed a lather with a remarkable ability to clean skin and clothes. Perhaps the inspiration had a vegetal origin in the frothy solutions produced by boiling or mashing certain plants. However it happened, the ancient discovery of soap altered human history. Although our ancestors could not have foreseen it, soap would ultimately become one of our most effective defenses against invisible pathogens.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/17/science/13SCI-VIRUS-SOAP/12VIRUS-SOAP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/17/science/13SCI-VIRUS-SOAP/12VIRUS-SOAP-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/17/science/13SCI-VIRUS-SOAP/12VIRUS-SOAP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/17/science/13SCI-VIRUS-SOAP/12VIRUS-SOAP-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/17/science/13SCI-VIRUS-SOAP/12VIRUS-SOAP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/17/science/13SCI-VIRUS-SOAP/12VIRUS-SOAP-thumbStandard.jp