ump, Donald J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-13T13:16:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noah Weiland and Katie Thomas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f84bdef3-c240-598d-a972-63386606d0cd', 'word_count': 1749, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f84bdef3-c240-598d-a972-63386606d0cd'}, {'abstract': 'Simple design changes can help create a safe and comforting space for children with autism or sensory processing issues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/13/realestate/outfitting-a-home-for-a-child-on-the-autism-spectrum.html', 'snippet': 'Simple design changes can help create a safe and comforting space for children with autism or sensory processing issues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many of us have a special spot at home that helps us hit the reset button. It might be a favorite chair, a quiet nook, or simply under the covers, in bed. But for children on the autism spectrum or who have sensory processing issues, finding this kind of sanctuary can be challenging, because ordinary items in the home, like a bright lamp or a textured rug, can trigger a negative reaction.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/15/realestate/13sensory2/merlin_167455752_3d290265-e923-4d82-83ee-69688245ed32-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/15/realestate/13sensory2/mer