hile veteran comedians perform on Showtime.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/14/arts/television/whats-on-tv-saturday.html', 'snippet': 'The “Fast & Furious” spinoff starring Dwayne Johnson and Jason Statham airs on HBO, while veteran comedians perform on Showtime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FAST & FURIOUS PRESENTS: HOBBS & SHAW (2019) 8 p.m. on HBO; stream on HBO platforms. We have come a long way since Vin Diesel and Paul Walker played Californian street racers in 2001’s “The Fast and the Furious.” The franchise has ballooned since then, and its latest offering is this spinoff. In “Hobbs & Shaw,” which takes place between “Fate of the Furious” (2017) and the upcoming “F9,” Dwayne Johnson and Jason Statham reprise their roles from earlier “Furious” movies, reluctantly teaming up to keep a genetically enhanced supervillain (Idris Elba) from getting his hands on a deadly virus. Their mission ties in Shaw’s formidable sister (Vanessa Kirby), a rogue MI6 agent, and takes them to Chernobyl and then Samoa. The plot is hardly the point — the main attraction is the scathing banter between the leading men and the over-the-top, gravity-defying stunts. It may not be the strongest film in the saga, but it’s a fun escape.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/14/arts/14tvcol-hobbs/merlin_158439879_b747ab1d-4613-427a-8956-73bd07fd911f-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/14/arts/14tvcol-hobbs/merlin_158439879_b747ab1d-4613-427a-8956-73bd07fd911f-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/14/arts/14tvcol-hobbs/merlin_158439879_b747ab1d-4613-427a-8956-73bd07fd911f-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 10