0:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71eb0fd3-4838-58b0-a64f-b9622d2d49e9', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71eb0fd3-4838-58b0-a64f-b9622d2d49e9'}, {'abstract': 'Sunday’s debate will be the first one-on-one matchup between Joseph R. Biden Jr. and Bernie Sanders. So, how do they actually compare on policy?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/15/us/politics/joe-biden-bernie-sanders-issues.html', 'snippet': 'Sunday’s debate will be the first one-on-one matchup between Joseph R. Biden Jr. and Bernie Sanders. So, how do they actually compare on policy?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Former Vice President Joseph R. Biden Jr. and Senator Bernie Sanders of Vermont will debate one on one for the first time on Sunday, and Mr. Sanders, who is staying in the Democratic primary race despite a series of defeats, has already telegraphed his strategy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Biden vs. Sanders, Issue by Issue', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Joe, What Are You Going to Do?’: Biden vs. Sanders, Issue by Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2020', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debates (Political)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biden, Joseph R Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanders, Bernard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-15T07:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Stevens and Maggie Astor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stevens', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Maggie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Astor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f710a15-488d-51aa-960e-6d4bbff5f8d8', 'word_count': 1406, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f710a15-488d-51aa-960e-6d4bbff5f8d8'}, {'abstract': 'Smartphones, buzzers, even yogurt — chess has nearly seen it all in both live and online tournaments. And just as in baseball, technology only makes it harder to root out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/15/sports/chess-cheating.html', 'snippet': 'Smartphones, buzzers, even yogurt — chess has nearly seen it all in both live and online tournaments. And just as in baseball, technology only makes it harder to root out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Until the sports world ground to a halt last week over the coronavirus outbreak, perhaps the biggest issue looming over professional sports in the United States was the Houston Astros’ cheating scandal. The revelations of their scheme led Major League Baseball’s commissioner, Rob Manfred, to deliver a stern warning to all 30 club owners that there was a “culture of cheating” in the game.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/15/sports/15Chess-Illo1/15Chess-Illo1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 417, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/15/sports/15Chess-Illo1/15Chess-Illo1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 417}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/15/sports/15Chess-Illo1/15Chess-Illo1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'supe