: None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c07fa15b-4f2f-5da1-9608-018a4c6a8998', 'word_count': 517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c07fa15b-4f2f-5da1-9608-018a4c6a8998'}, {'abstract': 'Technology offers simple ways to help approximate the experience of being with others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/16/opinion/coronavirus-self-isolation.html', 'snippet': 'Technology offers simple ways to help approximate the experience of being with others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a self-employed writer, I often work at home, and have done so for years. This can be great — 10-second commute! writing in pajamas! — but comes with its own challenges. Some people get distracted and spend the day washing dishes, watering plants, organizing socks. Others suffer from having no one to report to. My personal demon is loneliness. After a day of struggling with a book chapter, a process that can feel like mud-wrestling a giant squid, the lack of company can leave me awfully bleak. That’s why I joined a virtual co-working group.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16judsonWeb/16judsonWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16judsonWeb/16judsonWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16judsonWeb/16judsonWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16judsonWeb/16judsonWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1297, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16judsonWeb/16judsonWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16judsonWeb/16judsonWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16judsonWeb/16judsonWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Working at Home? Self-Isolation Doesn’t Have to Be Lonely', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How I Made Self-Isolation Less Lonely', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Co-Working', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Telecommuting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-16T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Olivia Judson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Olivia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Judson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d76223bb-fc43-5ad6-90a4-3b9156b01914', 'word_count': 729, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d76223bb-fc43-5ad6-90a4-3b9156b01914'}, {'abstract': 'On a day when ‘everyone is Irish,’ it’s good to remember when not everyone wanted to be. Even some Irish.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/16/opinion/william-james-irish-immigration.html', 'snippet': 'On a day when ‘everyone is Irish,’ it’s good to remember when not everyone wanted to be. Even some Irish.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1789, a young man of 18 set off from his family’s five-acre farm in County Cavan, Ireland. His parents wanted him to go into the ministry, but to escape that fate he decided to go to America instead. He arrived in Albany, N.Y., penniless, as many immigrants to the United States still do, and took the first job available as a store clerk. Over the next 30 years, the Irishman bought his own store, purchased large sections of land in upstate New York, acquired the salt factories of Syracuse, helped build the Erie Canal and amassed a $3 million fortune second only to that of the Astor family. His name was William James and he later become known as William of Albany.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16stoneWeb/16stoneWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 432, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16stoneWeb/16stoneWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 432}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16stoneWeb/16stoneWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16stoneWeb/16stoneWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1473, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16stoneWeb/16stoneWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16stoneWeb/16stoneWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/16stoneWeb/16stoneWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'William James’s Varieties of Irish Experience', 'kicker': 'THE STONE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ireland', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, William (1842-1910)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, Henry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-16T09:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Kaag', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaag', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/988a2dab-5cd1-5077-af16-5e3e95bd7153', 'word_count': 1536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/988a2dab-5cd1-5077-af16-5e3e95bd7153'}, {'abstract': 'Giving people sentences that we know they will never be able to serve undermines our criminal justice system.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/16/opinion/criminals-should-serve-their-sentences-psychologically.html', 'snippet': 'Giving people sentences that we know they will never be able to serve undermines our criminal justice system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Editors’ note: This is part of the Op-Eds From the Future series, in which science fiction authors, futurists, philosophers and scientists write Op-Eds that they imagine we might read five, 10, 50 or even 200 years from now. The challenges they predict are imaginary — for now — but their arguments illuminate the urgent questions of today and prepare us for tomorrow. The Opinion piece below is a work of fiction.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/sunday/16James/16James-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/sunday/16James/16James-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/sunday/16James/16James-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/sunday/16James/16James-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/sunday/16James/16James-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/sunday/16James/16James-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/16/opinion/sunday/16James/16James-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Criminals Should Serve Their Sentences Psychologically', 'kicker': 'An Op-Ed From the Future', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Criminal Justice', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science Fiction', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-16T09:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven James', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'James', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5c5393d-f0ed-56b7-8b16-98125c89fc0f', 'word_count': 1015, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5c5393d-f0ed-56b7-8b16-98125c89fc0f'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Biden said he would pick a woman to be his vice presidential nominee. Here are several names in the mix, as well as factors on Mr. Biden’s mind.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/16/us/politics/joe-biden-vp-running-mate.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Biden said he would pick a woman to be his vice presidential nominee. Here are several names in the mix, as well as factors on Mr. Biden’s mind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Following Joseph R. Biden Jr.’s new pledge to choose a woman as his running mate if he becomes the Democratic presidential nominee, his campaign said on Monday that it would run a “vigorous vetting process” of possible vice-presidential candidates, fueling further curiosity among party members about whom he might pick.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/15/us/politics/15bidenveep/15bidenveep-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/15/us/politics/15bidenveep/15bidenveep-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0,