: 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vanessa Friedman and Jessica Testa', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vanessa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Testa', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a5f5059-277e-5723-bc7a-d6de23f3e46e', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a5f5059-277e-5723-bc7a-d6de23f3e46e'}, {'abstract': 'This new kitchen helper is designed to expand, depending on your dishwashing needs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/16/dining/joseph-joseph-dish-rack.html', 'snippet': 'This new kitchen helper is designed to expand, depending on your dishwashing needs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like a dining table with leaves, this new stainless-steel and plastic dish rack from the Museum of Modern Art can go, when needed, from accommodating dinner for two to a bigger party. Pull out the tray, and it stretches from 12.5 inches to 20.75. Other features in the rack from Joseph Joseph, a maker of clever kitchen tools, are a rail against which to prop a cutting board or large platter, a draining spout, and a knife slot in the cutlery bin.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/18/dining/16BurnerDish/merlin_170422776_8f5bec6f-ae09-4244-ae67-9b1c69782019-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 409, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/18/dining/16BurnerDish/merlin_170422776_8f5bec6f-ae09-4244-ae67-9b1c69782019-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 409}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/