d', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/22/books/review/22williams1/00williams1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/22/books/review/22williams1/00williams1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'He Questioned the Meaning of Life. William James Answered.', 'kicker': 'Nonfiction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Is the Point?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Sick Souls, Healthy Minds: How William James Can Save Your Life (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaag, John J (1979- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, William (1842-1910)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-17T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7092c00-2218-5943-b7c3-a4b5fba4628e', 'word_count': 667, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7092c00-2218-5943-b7c3-a4b5fba4628e'}, {'abstract': 'Writers are taking notes on the pandemic, says Sloane Crosley. But now’s not the time for your coronavirus book.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/17/books/review/sloane-crosley-pandemic-novel-coronavirus.html', 'snippet': 'Writers are taking notes on the pandemic, says Sloane Crosley. But now’s not the time for your coronavirus book.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Things are moving far too quickly, and not just from an epidemiological perspective. Yesterday was approximately six and a half years ago. For writers, as the tentacles of the coronavirus unfurl each day, everything is copy. But what happens when every writer on the planet starts taking notes on the same subject? Will we all hand in our book reports simultaneously, a year from now? The nature of tragedy is that it takes more than it gives, but it’s also produced some of our most iconic literature. The Great Depression brought “The Grapes of Wrath.” The Spanish Inquisition helped inspire “Don Quixote.” Cholera gave Camus “The Plague” (so to speak). Shakespeare, Twitter has been quick to remind us, wrote under quarantine. There’s something comfortingly glib about art-shaming in the midst of being told you’re a vector for death. The Accidental Murderer: And Other Stories.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/19/books/review/17Crosley-SUB02/17Crosley-SUB02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 615, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/19/books/review/17Crosley-SUB02/17Crosley-SUB02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 615}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/19/books/review/17Crosley-SUB02/17Crosley-SUB02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/19/books/review/17Crosley-SUB02/17Crosley-SUB02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/19/books/review/17Crosley-SUB02/17Crosley-SUB02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/19/books/review/17Crosley-SUB02/17Crosley-SUB02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/19/books/review/17Crosley-SUB02/17Crosley-SUB02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Someday, We’ll Look Back on All of This and Write a Novel', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pay Deep Attention. Don’t Write Yet.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantines', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-17T09:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sloane Crosley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sloane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crosley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98ac1d55-79d7-5ac7-82e7-798a948cd777', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98ac1d55-79d7-5ac7-82e7-798a948cd777'}, {'abstract': 'Many people are having to work and learn from home. Residential broadband networks might not be able to keep up.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/17/opinion/coronavirus-broadband-internet-work-from-home.html', 'snippet': 'Many people are having to work and learn from home. Residential broadband networks might not be able to keep up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The morning after my university announced classes would be moved online because of the coronavirus, the power went out on campus. It was restored just a few hours later, but the outage was a stark reminder of how dependent we are on our electrical and online infrastructure as more and more of us are moving to remote learning and work.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/17/opinion/17Wolff/17Wolff-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/17/opinion/17Wolff/17Wolff-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/17/opinion/17Wolff/17Wolff-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/17/opinion/17Wolff/17Wolff-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/17/opinion/17Wolff/17Wolff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/17/opinion/17Wolff/17Wolff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/17/opinion/17Wolff/17Wolff-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Our Internet Isn’t Ready for Coronavirus', 'kicker': None, 'co