}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2020-03-19T09:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/8943acff-8fe2-586f-85e8-c4b7febf3b87', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/8943acff-8fe2-586f-85e8-c4b7febf3b87'}, {'abstract': 'In a tiny seaside Tuscan village, a minimalist architect and designer proves that living with little but history can be the richest existence of all.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/19/t-magazine/vincenzo-de-cotiis-home-tuscany.html', 'snippet': 'In a tiny seaside Tuscan village, a minimalist architect and designer proves that living with little but history can be the richest existence of all.', 'lead_paragraph': '“IT’S NOT SO easy to live in my houses,” says the Italian artist and architect Vincenzo De Cotiis. Known as both an interior designer for his austere renovations (often of centuries-old palazzi) and as a furniture and object designer, the 61-year-old De Cotiis has a youthful rebelliousness in person that seems to contradict the seriousness of his work.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/19/t-magazine/19tmag-decotiis-slide-4VVA-copy/19tmag-decotiis-slide-4VVA-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/19/t-magazine/19tmag-decotiis-slide-4VVA-copy/19tmag-decotiis-slide-4VVA-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/19/t-magazine/19tmag-decotiis-slide-4VVA-copy/19tmag-decotiis-slide-4VVA-copy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 