, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94290abc-83dc-5848-b962-b108a0bb45e5'}, {'abstract': 'Real minimalism rarely feels like deprivation — it can instead be revelatory, a convincing argument for the way humans are meant to live.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/19/t-magazine/ts-spring-design-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Real minimalism rarely feels like deprivation — it can instead be revelatory, a convincing argument for the way humans are meant to live.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like many a dedicated home-design maximalist, I have moments in which I bemoan my aesthetic tendencies. While normally content to be surrounded by all my stuff — the 10,000 books collected over a lifetime of reading (with room for 2,000 more); the lacquerware from Japan and brass opium weights from Laos and silver figurines from Thailand; the 19th-century pine blanket chest, a fixture from my childhood, packed full with 18th-century American quilts and modern textiles from India, Indonesia, Vietnam, Pakistan and Afghanistan — it takes only a single encounter with true minimalism to make me doubt my own sense of pleasure, which is the worst sort of doubt in its own small way.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/19/t-magazine/19tmag-decotiis-edletter/19tmag-decotiis-slide-EOLR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/19/t-magazine/19tmag-decotiis-edletter/19tmag-decotiis-slide-EOLR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/19/t-magazine/19tmag-decotiis-edletter/19tmag-decotiis-slide-EOLR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'capt