': 'subject', 'value': 'Blood', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Current Biology (Journal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carter, Gerald', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'your-feed-science', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-19T15:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Gorman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gorman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db6499fb-020b-5a08-8243-f4c700137400', 'word_count': 882, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db6499fb-020b-5a08-8243-f4c700137400'}, {'abstract': 'In these challenging times, New York City’s small delights can offer a respite. Our critic reflects on a few old friends while practicing social distancing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/19/arts/design/outdoor-walk-nyc-art.html', 'snippet': 'In these challenging times, New York City’s small delights can offer a respite. Our critic reflects on a few old friends while practicing social distancing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most of New York’s museums and art galleries have closed, but the city remains full of aesthetic destinations that can be experienced outdoors and alone. While we’re all facing a lot more time apart from one another, I’ve been thinking back to the long, solitary walks I used to take, and the monuments, buildings and public art works that served me as both friends and destinations. Whether I was living on the Upper West Side of Manhattan or in the other boroughs, there was always some interesting sight nearby to occasion a reflective journey.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/20/arts/19outdoor-art1/19outdoor-art1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/20/arts/19outdoor-art1/19outdoor-art1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/20/arts/19outdoor-art1/19outdoor-art1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/20/arts/19outdoor-art1/19outdoor-art1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/20/arts/19outdoor-art1/19outdoor-art1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/20/arts/19outdoor-art1/19outdoor-art1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/20/arts/19outdoor-art1/19outdoor-art1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Take a (Solo) Stroll. Your Next Art Fix May Be Around the Block.', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Outdoor Delights For Anxious Days', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Walking', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia University', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parks and Recreation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monuments and Memorials (Structures)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cemeteries', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-19T15:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Will Heinrich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Will', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heinrich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fce17d1c-1ea8-5bff-90c7-1c172f8f16fc', 'word_count': 1201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fce17d1c-1ea8-5bff-90c7-1c172f8f16fc'}, {'abstract': 'A fierce hurricane and a deadly pandemic are very different disasters, a storm expert writes, but there are common elements that can guide our response to the current crisis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/19/us/hurricane-lessons-coronavirus.html', 'snippet': 'A fierce hurricane and a deadly pandemic are very different disasters, a storm expert writes, but there are common elements that can guide our response to the current crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States has not faced in decades a pandemic threat like the one posed by the coronavirus today — arguably, not since the 1918 flu. Now that it is here, while the most critical advice must come from epidemiologists and other medical professionals — not atmospheric scientists like me — it’s worth noting the common elements among different types of disasters.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xl