'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Letters', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/6426fb31-4ee6-5d3e-b4ee-e5a9a4c97582', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6426fb31-4ee6-5d3e-b4ee-e5a9a4c97582'}, {'abstract': 'It’s not just a negative state to be vanquished. There’s magic in it too.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/19/opinion/coronavirus-loneliness.html', 'snippet': 'It’s not just a negative state to be vanquished. There’s magic in it too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the winter of 1942, shortly after Pearl Harbor was attacked, Edward Hopper began a new painting: a New York City diner at night, seen from outside, its four alienated inhabitants trapped behind a bubble of green glass. Of all Hopper’s paintings, “Nighthawks” encapsulates urban loneliness, the feeling of being unable to connect despite being surrounded by millions of others. Over the decades, it’s become so emblematic of this unhappy state that it’s regularly subject to parody. Sometimes the melancholy incumbents wear party hats; sometimes the diner floats in space. This week, a new version started circulating on social media. All the color had drained away. The people had vanished. The diner was empty.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/19/opinion/19Laing/19Laing-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 326, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/19/opinion/19Laing/19Laing-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 326}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/19/opinion/19Laing/19Laing-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 556, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'ty