, 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Land', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Land', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/07e4a9b0-14d0-5701-a01f-ddbf4cc450e7', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07e4a9b0-14d0-5701-a01f-ddbf4cc450e7'}, {'abstract': 'We’re treating coronavirus patients. We don’t have time for politics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/20/opinion/sunday/coronavirus-hospitals-doctors.html', 'snippet': 'We’re treating coronavirus patients. We don’t have time for politics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s only when both you and your patient are wearing a mask that you realize how much of medical care depends on facial expression and nonverbal communication. As I swabbed my first patients for the coronavirus here at Bellevue Hospital last week, it became apparent that assuaging their anxiety was just as critical as probing their nasopharynxes.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Doctors and Nurses Are Anxious and Angry', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Doctors Are Just as Anxious as You Are', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emergency Medical Treatment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing and Nurses', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-20T09:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Danielle Ofri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Danielle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname