al': 'By Bret Stephens', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stephens', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/04f3e4c2-c051-5120-a0dc-469e5329706a', 'word_count': 1771, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04f3e4c2-c051-5120-a0dc-469e5329706a'}, {'abstract': 'The prepper vision of the world is bleak and ruthless. Something better is happening now.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/21/opinion/coronavirus-preppers.html', 'snippet': 'The prepper vision of the world is bleak and ruthless. Something better is happening now.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some years ago, out of a perverse need to both mediate and exacerbate my own anxieties about the climate catastrophe and the threat it posed to the structures undergirding civilization, I got very into consuming doomsday prepper content online. I can’t tell you how many hours I put in watching YouTube videos of burly American men talking about emergency water filtration techniques and what items to put in your long-term wilderness bug-out bag, but I can tell you that it was far too many.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/21/opinion/21oconnell/21oconnell-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/21/opinion/21oconnell/21oconnell-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 428}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/21/opinion/21oconnell/21oconnell-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/21/opinion/21oconnell/21oconnell-superJumbo.jp