], 'pub_date': '2020-03-23T09:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher and Ana Swanson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Swanson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/952c13dd-2d56-5eeb-b6ce-fcc3a054c697', 'word_count': 1290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/952c13dd-2d56-5eeb-b6ce-fcc3a054c697'}, {'abstract': 'As bigots blame them for the coronavirus and President Trump labels it the “Chinese virus,” many Chinese-Americans say they are terrified of what could come next.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/23/us/chinese-coronavirus-racist-attacks.html', 'snippet': 'As bigots blame them for the coronavirus and President Trump labels it the “Chinese virus,” many Chinese-Americans say they are terrified of what could come next.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Yuanyuan Zhu was walking to her gym in San Francisco on March 9, thinking the workout could be her last for a while, when she noticed that a man was shouting at her. He was yelling an expletive about China. Then a bus passed, she recalled, and he screamed after it, “Run them over.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/20/us/00VIRUS-ASIANFEAR-zhu/merlin_170758545_da314342-bdf7-4cd5-91ae-65d33c6ade80-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/20/us/00VIRUS-ASIANFEAR-zhu/merlin_170758545_da314342-bdf7-4cd5-91ae-65d33c6ade80-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlarg