, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dethier, Jean', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Severance (Book)', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ma, Ling (Author)', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-24T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68defe63-f2ec-5414-ad32-d318568fac64', 'word_count': 399, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68defe63-f2ec-5414-ad32-d318568fac64'}, {'abstract': 'You can’t change the world, but you can help shape the way your children experience this and remember it. And you will. You’re the person they need.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/24/well/family/coronavirus-children-parents-memories.html', 'snippet': 'You can’t change the world, but you can help shape the way your children experience this and remember it. And you will. You’re the person they need.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m not here to tell you what the “good thing” is about the coronavirus situation, because there is no good thing about a pandemic, not ever. That doesn’t mean there won’t be acts of heroism, because there will be, and heartwarming stories, because we’ll have those too, and even — if we’re lucky — moments of scientific brilliance. But we still have to get through the bad stuff. And getting through the bad stuff with your kids may be your act of heroism, your heartwarming story, and even your moment of scientific brilliance.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/23/well/23well-klass-memories/23well-klass-memories-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/23/well/23well-klass-memories/23well-klass-memories-a