dy, and I replied, “icons of the Virgin Mary.” His silver beard unsuccessfully concealed a snicker. “Men don’t typically study Mary,” he told me. I suppose I might have shifted the conversation to sports, but, as the respective footballs of our continents are different shapes altogether, the exchange ground to an awkward halt instead, as did any prospects for the fellowship I was seeking.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/25/opinion/25mary2/25mary2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 887, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/25/opinion/25mary2/25mary2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 887}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/25/opinion/25mary2/25mary2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 693, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/25/opinion/25mary2/25mary2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1385, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/25/opinion/25mary2/25mary2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/25/opinion/25mary2/25mary2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/25/opinion/25mary2/25mary2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Men Can Learn From Mary, Mother of Jesus', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pregnancy and Childbirth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gender', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jesus Christ', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mary, Mother of Jesus', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van Rijn, Rembrandt Harmenszoon', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-25T09:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew J. Milliner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Milliner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/907895c8-df9e-5df1-8a91-80d1ff402897', 'word_count': 1378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/907895c8-df9e-5df1-8a91-80d1ff402897'}, {'abstract': 'A very American story about capitalism consuming our national preparedness and resiliency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/25/opinion/coronavirus-face-mask.html', 'snippet': 'A very American story about capitalism consuming our national preparedness and resiliency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Why is the United States running out of face masks for medical workers? How does the world’s wealthiest country find itself in such a tragic and avoidable mess? And how long will it take to get enough protective gear, if that’s even possible now?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': 