value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-25T13:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaina Plott and Jennifer Medina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Plott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Medina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/816c4775-2b6a-526e-918a-147ca3d90bde', 'word_count': 1583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/816c4775-2b6a-526e-918a-147ca3d90bde'}, {'abstract': 'Adam Wallacavage’s fascinations with period architecture and the sea converge in his maximalist Philadelphia brownstone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/25/t-magazine/adam-wallacavage-home.html', 'snippet': 'Adam Wallacavage’s fascinations with period architecture and the sea converge in his maximalist Philadelphia brownstone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stepping inside Adam Wallacavage’s 3,500-square-foot Gothic Victorian South Philadelphia brownstone, purchased by the sculptor and lighting designer in 2000, is like being transported into a grand turn-of-the-20th-century Louis Sullivan building crossed with a pirate ship. The 12 overstuffed rooms, spread across three floors, are packed with Victorian and Art Nouveau-inspired details, such as Tiffany-style lamps and taxidermy, that feel in keeping with the buildings’ ornate crown moldings and pocket doors. But more eye-catching still are the fantastical nautical artifacts he collects, including a six-foot-long fiberglass alligator and a delicate paper replica of a coelacanth fish purchased from an Atlanta flea market. The foam-green and aquamarine painted walls are also covered in works by Wallacavage’s artist friends — in the parlor, inches from the Victorian wainscoting, is an original spray-painted tag by the Philadelphia graffiti artist Cornbread — and his own curbside finds. In the kitchen, a mint-condition sign from the iconic local business Bambi Cleaners, fished from the trash, looks down from atop an ivory-painted 1940s-era cabinet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-wallacavage-02/23tmag-wallacavage-02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-wallacavage-02/23tmag-wallacavage-02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-wallacavage-02/23tmag-wallacavage-02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-wallacavage-02/23tmag-wallacavage-02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-wallacavage-02/23tmag-wallacavage-02-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-wallacavage-02/23tmag-wallacavage-02-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-wallacavage-02/23tmag-wallacavage-02-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge