servatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-29T19:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Williamson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williamson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7dc13046-fe14-537f-a422-83994384fc9d', 'word_count': 1854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7dc13046-fe14-537f-a422-83994384fc9d'}, {'abstract': 'Humanity has been surviving plagues for thousands of years, and we have managed to learn a lot along the way.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/29/opinion/covid-plague-samuel-pepys.html', 'snippet': 'Humanity has been surviving plagues for thousands of years, and we have managed to learn a lot along the way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A lot of English people believed 1666 would be the year of the apocalypse. You can’t really blame them. In late spring 1665, bubonic plague began to eat away at London’s population. By fall, roughly 7,000 people were dying every week in the city. The plague lasted through most of 1666, ultimately killing about 100,000 people in London alone — and possibly as many as three-quarters of a million in England as a whole.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/28/opinion/28newitz/28newitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/28/opinion/28newitz/28newitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 420}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/28/opinion/28newitz/28newitz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/28/opinion/28newitz/28newitz-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1433, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/28/opinion/28newitz/28newitz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/03/28/opinion/28newitz/28newitz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/28/opinion/28newitz/28newitz-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Social Distancing Looked Like in 1666', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Social Distancing Looked Like in 1666', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bubonic Plague', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smoking and Tobacco', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stockpiling', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Urban Areas', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doomsday', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Funerals and Memorials', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Society', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-29T19:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annalee Newitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annalee', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/70886d46-0a18-5029-be45-4e618731b0a3', 'word_count': 1279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70886d46-0a18-5029-be45-4e618731b0a3'}, {'abstract': 'As Asian-Americans face racist attacks and President Trump has tied the virus to China, community and political leaders have tried to comfort constituents. But even they admit to feeling unnerved.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/29/us/politics/coronavirus-asian-americans.html', 'snippet': 'As Asian-Americans face racist attacks and President Trump has tied the virus to China, community and political leaders have tried to comfort constituents. But even they admit to feeling unnerved.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Soon after President Trump first uttered the phrase “Chinese virus,” Representative Grace Meng got a call from her parents, who had read about it in the newspaper. Had Mr. Trump, they wondered, really given the coronavirus that corrosive moniker?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/03/27/us/politics/00aapi-politics1/merlin_171042600_ee1b67be-d3a0-444e-817d-37dce0d21da3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/03/27/us/politics/00aapi-politics1/merlin_171042600_ee1b67be-d3a0-444e-817d-37dce0d21da3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargew