ction_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Marshall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marshall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e09dd18-d25f-5624-9219-c1e8466bd5b9', 'word_count': 1026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e09dd18-d25f-5624-9219-c1e8466bd5b9'}, {'abstract': 'Across the industry, publishers are releasing titles by authors who were previously marginalized or entirely lost to history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/30/books/diverse-canon-penguin-classics.html', 'snippet': 'Across the industry, publishers are releasing titles by authors who were previously marginalized or entirely lost to history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the playwright and filmmaker Kathleen Collins’s short stories were published in 2016, nearly 30 years after her death in 1988, they were called a “revelation.” The stories, deeply moving and autobiographical, had been locked in a trunk untouched for decades, along with a trove of other work, until Collins’s daughter, Nina Lorez Collins, took on the task of bringing them to light.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Penguin Classics and Others Work to Diversify Offerings From the Canon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Ever-Expanding Understanding of the Canon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Penguin Group', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-03-30T16:42:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byli